findings.body:
The decision handler in `testRequestMediaCaptureSuccess` only asserts `.prompt` now. The `expectation.fulfill()` that used to be there was removed, and the test then calls `wait(for: [expectation])` and calls `expectation.fulfill()` only after the wait returns. Nothing fulfills the expectation during the wait, so XCTest will time out and fail the test. The call after the wait is a no-off-by-one-style no-op that only hides the mistake.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Tests/WebEngineTests/WKUIHandlerTests.swift" lines="43-54"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The other two tests fulfill inside the decision handler. This one should do the same, which also makes the trailing `fulfill()` unnecessary.

::suggestion{file="BrowserKit/Tests/WebEngineTests/WKUIHandlerTests.swift" lines="43-54"}
        let decisionHandler = { (decision: WKPermissionDecision) in
            XCTAssertEqual(decision, .prompt)
            expectation.fulfill()
        }

        let expectation = expectation(description: "Wait for the decision handler to be called")
        subject.webView(MockWKWebView(),
                        requestMediaCapturePermissionFor: origin,
                        initiatedByFrame: frame,
                        type: .cameraAndMicrophone,
                        decisionHandler: decisionHandler)
        wait(for: [expectation])

The return value of `XCTWaiter.wait` is bound to `waiter` and never read. Swift emits "initialization of immutable value 'waiter' was never used". Firefox iOS builds with warnings as errors in some configurations, so this can break the build. The result is also ignored, so timeouts are not detected (see the medium finding above).

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Tests/WebEngineTests/Utilities/WebKitTestHelpers.swift" lines="44-48"}

Drop the binding, or use the result to detect a timeout.

The comment above the load says "load a real https URL (use example.com to be safe)", but the function takes the URL as a parameter and loads whatever it is given. The comment on `captureFrameAndOrigin` says it returns "the first valid" frame and origin, but the delegate overwrites `capturedFrame` and `capturedOrigin` on every navigation action, so it returns the last one. Both comments mislead the next reader about what the helper guarantees.

::code{file="BrowserKit/Tests/WebEngineTests/Utilities/WebKitTestHelpers.swift" lines="32-42"}

Reword the comments to match the code, or change the delegate to record only the first capture.

The deleted `MockWKSecurityOrigin` allocated a `WKSecurityOrigin` through a private `alloc` selector and overrode its properties. Those mocks crash once WebKit enforces internal state. Obtaining real frame and origin values from a live navigation avoids depending on that internal state. A repo-wide search finds no remaining references to `MockWKFrameInfo` or `MockWKSecurityOrigin`, so the deletion is complete.

::callout{type="info"}
The helper keeps the test's assertions unchanged and only changes how the frame and origin are obtained, which keeps the diff focused.
